# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

Use this resource when you want to expose part (or all) of a bucket to anonymous clients. It builds on the standard bucket policy primitives while adding conveniences for canned policies and importable state.

> **Important:** For a given bucket, manage anonymous access with either this resource _or_ `minio_s3_bucket_policy`—not both. They share the same underlying bucket policy and will constantly fight each other if applied simultaneously.

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/resources/minio_s3_bucket_anonymous_access/resource.tf" }}

- `access_type` supports MinIO canned modes (`public`, `public-read`, `public-read-write`, `public-write`).
- `policy` accepts any valid S3/MinIO policy JSON. When both `policy` and `access_type` are set, the explicit JSON policy always wins.

### What `public` grants

`access_type = "public"` gives anonymous clients full access to the bucket's **data** and nothing else:

- On the bucket: `s3:GetBucketLocation`, `s3:ListBucket`, `s3:ListBucketMultipartUploads`.
- On its objects: `s3:GetObject`, `s3:PutObject`, `s3:DeleteObject`, `s3:AbortMultipartUpload`, `s3:ListMultipartUploadParts`.

It grants no administrative action, so an anonymous client cannot read or rewrite the bucket policy, delete the bucket, or subscribe to its event notifications. If you need any of those, write them out yourself in `policy`.

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Import

Import is supported using the following syntax:

```shell
# Anonymous access is keyed by bucket name
terraform import minio_s3_bucket_anonymous_access.example bucket-name
```
